Chapter: LirBranchCross

  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter MathLib

 The shapes the ARM64 LIR selector claims at step 4e: a comparison, a
 conditional branch, and a return in each arm. `mn` lowers to
 `b0: cmp v0 v1; br lt b1 b2 | b1: ret v0 | b2: ret v1`, which is the first
 multi-block function this backend emits through the LIR and so the first with
 a block-offset table and branch fixups behind it.

 Every case here is chosen so a selector can fail it without failing the rest.
 `mn` and `mx` differ only in the condition, so a wrong condition code answers
 the other one's result. `mn-eq` and `mx-eq` feed equal arguments, which is the
 boundary the strict conditions get wrong in the direction the inverse would:
 `<` and `<=` disagree on exactly this input and nowhere else. `sgn` and
 `sgn-neg` compare against an immediate. `far-cmp` compares against a value
 past ARM64's twelve-bit immediate field, so the operand has to be materialised
 into a scratch register rather than encoded.

 `nest` is the shape whose block ids are not in layout order: the lowering
 numbers the inner arms above the outer join, so it lays out b0, b1, b4, b5,
 b6, b2, b3 and an offset table indexed by list position rather than by id
 would send its branches to the wrong place.

 `bit-even` and `bit-mod4` are here as the control that the selector declines
 politely. Both lower to a call, which is not whitelisted, so both go to the
 tree emitter and must still answer correctly beside the functions that do not.
 They also carry negative arguments, because a remainder and a bit mask do not
 agree in general and only agree against zero.

 **`wide-branch` does NOT currently exercise this construct, and the row is kept
 as an ordinary correctness check rather than as coverage.** It was written to
 combine a frame with a branch -- eight parameters, enough live values to reach
 the callee-saved pool, so each arm's return would have to undo the frame it was
 entered with. Measured, it does not: perturbing the branch condition inverter
 breaks every other selected shape in this file and leaves `wide-t` and
 `wide-f` correct, so the ARM64 selector is declining it and the tree emitter is
 answering. It is clean under the compiler's own ARM64-shaped probe descriptor
 (`[alloc-check: ok]`, `[check: ok]`), so the verifiers are not what refuse it;
 the plug lowers the IR it receives over the wire with its own
 `lower-def-to-lir`, and that is where the two disagree.

 **So frame-plus-branch is an untested combination**, and it is named here
 rather than implied. `codex/test/lir-frame-cross` covers the frame and the
 shapes above cover the branch; nothing yet covers a return that has to undo a
 frame.

Section: Branch Shapes

  mn : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  mn (a) (b) = if a < b then a else b

  mx : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  mx (a) (b) = if a > b then a else b

  le-pick : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  le-pick (a) (b) = if a <= b then 10 else 20

  ge-pick : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ge-pick (a) (b) = if a >= b then 30 else 40

  ne-pick : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ne-pick (a) (b) = if a /= b then 50 else 60

  sgn : Integer -> Integer
  sgn (n) = if n > 0 then 1 else 0

  far-cmp : Integer -> Integer
  far-cmp (n) = if n > 100000 then 7 else 8

  nest : Integer -> Integer
  nest (n) = if n > 0 then (if n > 5 then 2 else 1) else 0

  bit-even : Integer -> Integer
  bit-even (n) = if math-mod n 2 == 0 then 1 else 0

  bit-mod4 : Integer -> Integer
  bit-mod4 (n) = if math-mod n 4 == 0 then 1 else 0

  wide-branch : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wide-branch (a) (b) (c) (d) (e) (f) (g) (h) =
   if ((a + b) + (c + d)) > ((e + f) + (g + h))
   then (((a * b) + (c * d)) + ((e * f) + (g * h)))
   else (((a - b) + (c - d)) + ((e - f) + (g - h)))

  via-call : Integer -> Integer
  via-call (n) = mn n 4 + mx n 4 + nest n

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("mn: " & show (mn 3 9))
    print-line-uni ("mn-rev: " & show (mn 9 3))
    print-line-uni ("mn-eq: " & show (mn 5 5))
    print-line-uni ("mx: " & show (mx 3 9))
    print-line-uni ("mx-eq: " & show (mx 5 5))
    print-line-uni ("le-lt: " & show (le-pick 3 9))
    print-line-uni ("le-eq: " & show (le-pick 5 5))
    print-line-uni ("le-gt: " & show (le-pick 9 3))
    print-line-uni ("ge-eq: " & show (ge-pick 5 5))
    print-line-uni ("ge-lt: " & show (ge-pick 3 9))
    print-line-uni ("ne-same: " & show (ne-pick 5 5))
    print-line-uni ("ne-diff: " & show (ne-pick 5 6))
    print-line-uni ("sgn-pos: " & show (sgn 4))
    print-line-uni ("sgn-zero: " & show (sgn 0))
    print-line-uni ("far-lo: " & show (far-cmp 5))
    print-line-uni ("far-hi: " & show (far-cmp 200000))
    print-line-uni ("nest-hi: " & show (nest 9))
    print-line-uni ("nest-mid: " & show (nest 3))
    print-line-uni ("nest-lo: " & show (nest 0))
    print-line-uni ("even: " & show (bit-even 4))
    print-line-uni ("odd: " & show (bit-even 5))
    print-line-uni ("even-neg: " & show (bit-even (-4)))
    print-line-uni ("odd-neg: " & show (bit-even (-5)))
    print-line-uni ("mod4: " & show (bit-mod4 8))
    print-line-uni ("mod4-no: " & show (bit-mod4 6))
    print-line-uni ("wide-t: " & show (wide-branch 9 8 1 1 1 1 1 1))
    print-line-uni ("wide-f: " & show (wide-branch 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8))
    print-line-uni ("via-call: " & show (via-call 7))
  end
